\paragraph{Synthesis.}
Image tokens remain instruction-blind across all 32 layers (causal mask, frozen encoder); the readout token is the sole multimodal convergence point.
VLA fine-tuning shifts routing from mid-depth (L9--L16) to entry-exit (L0--L1, L31), concentrating effects at the readout ($R^2$ $+0.069$) rather than image representations ($\Delta \leq 1.7\%$).

\paragraph{Mechanism-behavior connection.}
Image-token instruction-blindness explains the visual dominance reported by Grant et al.~\cite{grant2026}: actions rely on readout-token conditioning.
Under RSA, DINOv2 achieves the highest geometric alignment ($\rho = 0.692$), yet VLA readout surpasses it in decodability ($R^2 = 0.678$ vs.\ $0.634$), indicating instruction-conditioned integration rather than enhanced geometry.

\paragraph{Architectural implications.}
L1 attention ablation confirms head specialization: removing instruction attention redirects to image tokens, recovering action encoding ($R^2$ $+46\%$, $p < 0.0001$), with deep layers unaffected ($|\Delta R^2| < 0.01$).

\paragraph{Limitations and scope.}
We study OpenVLA-7B on LIBERO; generalization to other VLAs (e.g., RT-2~\cite{brohan2023_rt2}, $\pi_0$~\cite{black2024_pi0}) and backbones remains open.
Per-layer profiles are metric-dependent (RSA vs.\ CKA; Appendix~\ref{sec:cka_appendix}); $R^2$ provides metric-independent evidence.
The L1 ablation uses 5 demonstrations per task; analyses are post-hoc (Appendix~\ref{sec:metric_sensitivity_appendix}).

